'''Bohíque''' was the Guaji term for a ritual specialist, healer, teacher, and keeper of sacred records. Bohíques maintained the [[Bikumaku]] archive, performed divination, supervised seasonal ceremonies, and prepared herbal remedies. In pre-colonial Guajicutea, they formed the practical religious class below the [[Warí]] — the high priestess — and helped carry out the daily work of the island's sacred system.

The bohíque office was central to the [[Warí faith in Guajicutea|traditional Guaji religion]]. Bohíques interpreted dreams, storms, and omens; recited ritual histories; supervised parts of the lunar calendar; and advised villages on planting, travel, healing, and funerary practice. Their authority came less from political office than from mastery of ritual knowledge and long apprenticeship.

== Role ==

Bohíques occupied a middle position in Guaji sacred life. The Warí held the highest spiritual authority, especially in disputes over doctrine and the ritual order, but bohíques carried out much of the practical work of religion. They taught sacred formulas, cared for ritual objects, and ensured that ceremonies followed the proper sequence.

In village life, a bohíque might serve as a healer, calendrical advisor, or ritual keeper. In larger centres they worked with catacomb records, temple rites, and public festivals. Their responsibilities touched nearly every part of the old Guaji religious order, from the timing of fishing and travel to the treatment of illness and the marking of death.

== Training and knowledge ==

Bohíques were trained through long apprenticeship. A child or adolescent selected for the religious track would live with a master practitioner and learn sacred recitation, ritual order, herbal medicine, carving, calendar reckoning, and the interpretation of omens. Knowledge was transmitted orally, by imitation, and through work with carved records and ceremonial objects.

Because the Guaji religious system tied together sea, mountain, and sky, bohíques needed to understand both the physical environment and the sacred meanings attached to it. They were expected to know the meaning of storms, tides, disease, crop cycles, and funerary custom. A bohíque who could not read the season correctly or prepare a healing remedy effectively was not fully trusted.

== Ritual duties ==

Bohíques were involved in many of the island's most important rituals. They helped maintain the lunar calendar, interpreted the carved year-marks in the catacombs, and supervised ceremonies linked to planting, voyaging, and the dead. After the shift to cremation in 516 CE, they also helped manage the ash niches used for elite burials and the ritual boundaries between the living and the underworld.

They were also guardians of historical memory. The calendar corridor and storm ledger in the [[Catacumbas del Abismo]] were not just archives but sacred documents, and bohíques were the specialists who preserved, read, and updated them. In that sense, the office sat at the intersection of religion, history, and public memory.

== Colonial period ==

The arrival of Burawi Christianity in the 16th century destabilised the bohíque order. Missionaries tried to replace Guaji ritual with Christian practice, and the new religious hierarchy reduced the public authority of bohíques. Some ritual knowledge was absorbed into local Christian practice, especially herbal medicine and ceremonial language, but the office itself was pushed to the margins.

The burden fell especially hard during the religious fracture of the colonial period, when Guaji priests and priestesses were pressured to abandon older practices. Many bohíques resisted, some continued in secret, and others shifted into quieter roles as healers or keepers of family tradition. The office survived, but it lost the institutional centrality it had held before colonisation.

== Modern use ==

In modern Guajicutea, '''bohíque''' is used mainly as a historical and revivalist term. It appears in scholarship, cultural restoration, and the modern revival of [[Warí faith in Guajicutea|traditional Guaji religion]]. Some speakers also use it more loosely for respected ritual experts or healers in rural settings, though the old office no longer has the same institutional power.

The term remains important because it names the religious specialists who preserved the island's sacred records, calendar knowledge, and healing traditions for centuries. Without the bohíques, much of the Guaji ritual archive would have been far harder to maintain.

